export const DEFAULT_RESEARCH_PAPER_HTML = "<h1>Comparative Dynamic Modelling, Design, and Sustainability Optimization of CSTR and PFR Systems for Biological Treatment of Textile Wastewater: A Literature-Calibrated Digital Twin Approach</h1>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> Research Engineering Group (Project 24)<br />\n<strong>Academic Affiliation:</strong> Department of Chemical Engineering<br />\n<strong>Subject:</strong> Chemical Reaction Engineering (CRE) — Biological Reactor Design<br />\n<strong>Date:</strong> November 2026  </p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Executive Abstract</h2>\n<p>Biological treatment of industrial textile wastewater poses severe reaction-engineering challenges due to the presence of recalcitrant synthetic dyestuffs, high salinity, surfactants, and variable organic loads. This research presents a rigorous, literature-calibrated comparative design and operational sustainability assessment between an <strong>ideal Continuous Stirred-Tank Reactor (CSTR)</strong> and an <strong>ideal Plug-Flow Reactor (PFR)</strong> equipped with cell recycle. The biological reaction kinetics are formulated using an <strong>advanced dual-limitation Monod model</strong> incorporating active heterotrophic microbial growth, endogenous decay, solids retention time (<span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub></span>), and dissolved oxygen limitation (<span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub> = 3.2 day<sup>-1</sup></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub> = 65.0 mg COD/L</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">Y = 0.45 g VSS/g COD</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">b = 0.06 day<sup>-1</sup></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>O</sub> = 0.20 mg O<sub>2</sub>/L</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">k<sub>L</sub>a = 120.0 day<sup>-1</sup></span>). </p>\n<p>Governing differential material balances were derived following <strong>Fogler’s reaction engineering methodology</strong> and integrated numerically using high-order stiff solvers (<code>SciPy Radau</code>). For a baseline industrial finishing mill discharging <span class=\"math-inline\">Q = 1000 m<sup>3</sup>/day</span> with an influent biodegradable Chemical Oxygen Demand (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub></span>, bCOD) of <span class=\"math-inline\">750 mg/L</span> and a target effluent standard of <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> ≤ 75 mg/L</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">90.0%</span> bCOD conversion), the required reactor working volume is <strong><span class=\"math-inline\">489.7 m<sup>3</sup></span></strong> for the CSTR (Hydraulic Retention Time <span class=\"math-inline\">τ = 11.75 h</span>) and <strong><span class=\"math-inline\">494.8 m<sup>3</sup></span></strong> for the PFR (<span class=\"math-inline\">τ = 11.87 h</span>), yielding a volume ratio of <span class=\"math-inline\">0.99×</span> at matched biological solids inventory (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub> ≈ 3877 mg VSS/L</span>). Operational sustainability analysis indicates an energy consumption of <strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.445 kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span></strong> ($0.0423/m³, <span class=\"math-inline\">0.274 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/m<sup>3</sup></span>) for the CSTR versus <strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.447 kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span></strong> ($0.0425/m³, <span class=\"math-inline\">0.275 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/m<sup>3</sup></span>) for the PFR. The numerical engine was independently benchmarked against analytical first-order limiting cases (<span class=\"math-inline\">V<sub>CSTR</sub>/V<sub>PFR</sub> = 3.91</span> at <span class=\"math-inline\">90%</span> conversion). These results demonstrate that while classical chemical kinetics favors tubular reactors for non-inhibited systems, biological activated-sludge systems operated under controlled cell retention exhibit near-hydrodynamic parity, with the CSTR providing superior operational stability against upstream toxic shocks.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> Chemical Reaction Engineering, Biological Wastewater Treatment, Monod Kinetics, CSTR, PFR, Textile Wastewater, COD Fractionation, Digital Twin, Sustainability Assessment.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Nomenclature and Unit System</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Symbol</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Definition</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Engineering Unit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">SI Equivalent</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">S</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Biodegradable soluble substrate concentration (bCOD)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg COD/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/m<sup>3</sup></span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">10<sup>-3</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup></span>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Influent biodegradable substrate concentration</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg COD/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Effluent biodegradable substrate concentration</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg COD/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Active heterotrophic biomass concentration</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg VSS/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">DO</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bulk liquid dissolved oxygen concentration</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg O<sub>2</sub>/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">DO<sup>*</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Saturated dissolved oxygen concentration at <span class=\"math-inline\">20°C</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg O<sub>2</sub>/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">8.5 × 10<sup>-3</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">Q</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volumetric wastewater influent flow rate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">m<sup>3</sup>/day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.157 × 10<sup>-2</sup> m<sup>3</sup>/s</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">V</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reactor liquid working volume</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">τ</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hydraulic retention time (<span class=\"math-inline\">V/Q</span>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">hours</span> or <span class=\"math-inline\">days</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">seconds</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">86400 s/day</span>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Mean cell residence time / Solids retention time (SRT)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">days</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">seconds</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Maximum specific microbial growth rate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.157 × 10<sup>-5</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Half-velocity constant for biodegradable substrate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg COD/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>O</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Oxygen half-saturation constant</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg O<sub>2</sub>/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">Y</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Heterotrophic biomass yield coefficient</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g VSS / g bCOD</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">dimensionless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">b</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Endogenous microbial decay coefficient</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.157 × 10<sup>-6</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">k<sub>L</sub>a</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volumetric oxygen mass transfer coefficient</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">s<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">α<sub>O</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Net oxygen demand coefficient for substrate oxidation</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g O<sub>2</sub> / g bCOD</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">dimensionless (<span class=\"math-inline\">0.50</span>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">OUR</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volumetric Oxygen Uptake Rate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg O<sub>2</sub> / (L · day)</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">g/(m<sup>3</sup> · day)</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">OTR</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volumetric Oxygen Transfer Rate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kg O<sub>2</sub> / day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kg/s</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>S</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fractional substrate conversion (<span class=\"math-inline\">[S<sub>0</sub> - S<sub>e</sub>]/S<sub>0</sub></span>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">dimensionless</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0 ≤ X<sub>S</sub> ≤ 1</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">η<sub>S</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Percentage biodegradable substrate removal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">%</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">100 × X<sub>S</sub></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>1. Introduction and Project Motivation</h2>\n<p>Industrial textile wet processing (scouring, desizing, bleaching, mercerizing, dyeing, and finishing) is among the most water- and chemical-intensive industrial sectors globally (Vandevivere et al., 1998; Robinson et al., 2001). A typical wet-processing mill generates between <span class=\"math-inline\">100</span> and <span class=\"math-inline\">250 m<sup>3</sup></span> of wastewater per ton of finished fabric, characterized by elevated Chemical Oxygen Demand (<span class=\"math-inline\">COD = 800 - 2500 mg/L</span>), intense coloration from synthetic azo and anthraquinone reactive dyes, fluctuating <span class=\"math-inline\">pH</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">6 - 11</span>), high electrical conductivity (<span class=\"math-inline\">2000 - 6000 μS/cm</span>), and xenobiotic auxiliary agents (Germirli Babuna et al., 1999; Orhon et al., 2003; dos Santos et al., 2007).</p>\n<p>In modern environmental engineering practice, biological activated-sludge treatment represents the core secondary unit process due to its operational cost-effectiveness compared to purely physicochemical or electrochemical technologies (Metcalf &amp; Eddy, 2014; Chen et al., 2020). However, textbook Chemical Reaction Engineering (CRE) formulations derived for homogeneous chemical syntheses (Levenspiel, 1999; Fogler, 2016) demonstrate that an ideal tubular <strong>Plug-Flow Reactor (PFR)</strong> consistently requires a substantially smaller reactor volume than an ideal <strong>Continuous Stirred-Tank Reactor (CSTR)</strong> for any positive reaction order (<span class=\"math-inline\">n > 0</span>) achieving high conversions (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>S</sub> > 85%</span>). </p>\n<p>In biochemical environmental processes, this classical thermodynamic deduction is fundamentally complicated by two coupled physical mechanisms:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Biological Autocatalytic Growth &amp; Solids Retention:</strong> Heterotrophic bacteria are both the reaction catalyst and the self-replicating reaction product. In suspended-growth continuous systems, active biomass must be actively retained or recycled via secondary clarifiers; otherwise, hydrodynamic washout occurs whenever dilution rate exceeds specific growth rate (<span class=\"math-inline\">D > μ<sub>net</sub></span>) (Lawrence &amp; McCarty, 1970; Grady et al., 2011).</li>\n<li><strong>Dissolved Oxygen Transfer Limitation:</strong> Microorganisms require molecular oxygen as the terminal electron acceptor. Aeration is governed by gas-liquid mass transfer rate (<span class=\"math-inline\">k<sub>L</sub>a</span>) and biological oxygen demand (<span class=\"math-inline\">OUR</span>). Substrate utilization rate cannot exceed available oxygen transfer without inducing anaerobic septic conditions (Rosso &amp; Stenstrom, 2006; Baquero-Rodriguez et al., 2018).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>To visualize these two contrasting engineering configurations, the complete process flow diagrams for both systems are contrasted in <strong>Figure 1</strong>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 1: Process Flow Schematics of CSTR and PFR\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig1_reactor_schematic.png\" />\n<em>Figure 1: Process flow diagrams of the biological activated-sludge systems under comparison: (a) Continuous Stirred-Tank Reactor (CSTR) characterized by instantaneous bulk micro-mixing (<span class=\"math-inline\">C<sub>exit</sub> = C<sub>bulk</sub></span>) with bottom-diffused bubble aeration (<span class=\"math-inline\">k<sub>L</sub>a</span>), secondary clarifier, and Return Activated Sludge (RAS) loop governing mean cell residence time (<span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub></span>); (b) Plug-Flow Reactor (PFR) characterized by 1D axial advection with zero back-mixing (<span class=\"math-inline\">Pe  →  ∈fty</span>) coupled with an inlet biomass recycle loop (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b,0</sub></span>) to sustain steady biological inventory.</em></p>\n<p>The specific research objectives are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conduct a traceable literature review across 50 peer-reviewed investigations to extract validated Monod kinetic parameters, COD fractionation data, and aeration parameters for textile biological treatment.</li>\n<li>Formulate from first conservation principles the coupled differential material balances for substrate (<span class=\"math-inline\">S</span>), active biomass (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub></span>), and dissolved oxygen (<span class=\"math-inline\">DO</span>) in ideal CSTR and PFR systems following Fogler’s methodology.</li>\n<li>Establish a physically consistent biomass retention model for PFR systems that accounts for solids return (RAS) to avoid unrealistic washout artifacts.</li>\n<li>Develop and verify a high-accuracy numerical simulation engine in Python (<code>SciPy Radau</code>), validated against analytical limiting solutions.</li>\n<li>Perform comparative reactor sizing, sensitivity analysis, and operational sustainability evaluation (energy consumption, operating cost, and carbon footprint).</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>2. Comprehensive Literature Review &amp; Kinetic Synthesis</h2>\n<h3>2.1 Literature Search Methodology and PRISMA Flowchart</h3>\n<p>Following the PRISMA 2020 protocol for reproducible scientific literature extraction, peer-reviewed databases (Scopus, Web of Science, OpenAlex, and Semantic Scholar) were systematically queried. As detailed in the PRISMA flowchart in <strong>Figure 2</strong>, a total of 412 candidate records were screened, resulting in 50 fully verified peer-reviewed publications covering textile effluent characterization, Monod kinetics, hydrodynamics, oxygen transfer, and life-cycle sustainability.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 2: PRISMA 2020 Flowchart\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig2_prisma_flowchart.png\" />\n<em>Figure 2: PRISMA 2020 literature search and screening workflow showing identification of 412 records, duplicate removal, title/abstract screening, full-text eligibility appraisal, and final inclusion of 50 verified peer-reviewed studies categorized into five distinct reaction engineering thematic clusters.</em></p>\n<h3>2.2 Textile Wastewater Characterization and COD Fractionation</h3>\n<p>A major pitfall in industrial wastewater modeling is the assumption that total Chemical Oxygen Demand (<span class=\"math-inline\">COD<sub>tot</sub></span>) is entirely biodegradable. In textile wet-processing effluent, total COD is divided into distinct fractions governed by the Activated Sludge Models (Henze et al., 2000; Orhon et al., 2003):\n<div class=\"math-block\">COD<sub>tot</sub> = S<sub>s</sub> + X<sub>s</sub> + S<sub>i</sub> + X<sub>i</sub></div>\nwhere <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>s</sub></span> is soluble readily biodegradable substrate, <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>s</sub></span> is particulate slowly biodegradable substrate requiring extracellular enzymatic hydrolysis, <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>i</sub></span> is soluble inert non-biodegradable COD, and <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>i</sub></span> is particulate inert COD.</p>\n<p>Seminal experimental investigations on knit fabric and cotton finishing wastewater by Orhon et al. (1992, 1999, 2003) demonstrated that textile wastewater exhibits an initial soluble inert fraction (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>i</sub></span>) ranging between <span class=\"math-inline\">80</span> and <span class=\"math-inline\">180 mg/L</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">10 - 18%</span> of total COD). Furthermore, microbial metabolic activity produces residual soluble microbial products (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>p</sub> ≈ 4 - 8%</span> of initial substrate), establishing an absolute refractory baseline below which biological treatment cannot proceed without tertiary membrane separation or advanced oxidation (Oller et al., 2011; Yurtsever et al., 2015). Consequently, the biologically assimilable substrate is defined as <strong>biodegradable soluble COD (<span class=\"math-inline\">S</span>, bCOD)</strong>:\n<div class=\"math-block\">S = S<sub>s</sub> + f<sub>H</sub> X<sub>s</sub></div>\nwhere the biodegradable fraction <span class=\"math-inline\">f<sub>b</sub> = bCOD / COD<sub>tot</sub></span> typically ranges between <span class=\"math-inline\">0.55</span> and <span class=\"math-inline\">0.72</span> for desized textile effluents (Baban et al., 2003; Karahan et al., 2008).</p>\n<h3>2.3 Monod Growth Kinetics and Rate Transitions</h3>\n<p>The degradation of biodegradable organic substrate by aerobic heterotrophic biomass is classically governed by Monod kinetics (Monod, 1949):\n<div class=\"math-block\">μ(S) = μ<sub>max</sub> (S) / (K<sub>S</sub> + S)</div>\nIn textile effluents containing inhibitory dyes and salts, maximum specific growth rates (<span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub></span>) are observed to be lower (<span class=\"math-inline\">1.8 - 4.2 day<sup>-1</sup></span>) and half-saturation affinity constants (<span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub></span>) significantly higher (<span class=\"math-inline\">40 - 120 mg COD/L</span>) than in municipal sewage (<span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub> ≈ 6.0 day<sup>-1</sup></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub> ≈ 20 mg/L</span>) due to xenobiotic mass-transfer resistance and dye toxicity (Kapdan &amp; Kargi, 2002; Sponza &amp; Isik, 2002; Guan et al., 2004; Isik &amp; Sponza, 2004). </p>\n<p>The dual-limitation Monod behavior and the transition between kinetic regimes are plotted in <strong>Figure 3</strong>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 3: Monod Kinetics and Rate Regimes\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig3_monod_kinetics.png\" />\n<em>Figure 3: Dual-limitation Monod kinetics for textile wastewater biodegradation: (a) Specific microbial growth rate <span class=\"math-inline\">μ(S, DO)</span> as a function of biodegradable substrate concentration <span class=\"math-inline\">S</span> across dissolved oxygen levels (<span class=\"math-inline\">DO = 0.1</span> to <span class=\"math-inline\">5.0 mg/L</span>), identifying the half-velocity affinity constant <span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub> = 65 mg/L</span> and asymptotic ceiling <span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub> = 3.2 day<sup>-1</sup></span>; (b) Substrate consumption rate <span class=\"math-inline\">-r<sub>S</sub></span> showing the extensive zero-order saturation plateau at elevated industrial concentrations (<span class=\"math-inline\">S ≫ K<sub>S</sub></span>) and the first-order transition occurring only as substrate approaches low residual concentrations (<span class=\"math-inline\">S ≤ K<sub>S</sub></span>).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Table 1: Literature-Calibrated Biological Kinetic and Stoichiometric Parameters</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Parameter</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Symbol</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Nominal Value</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Range in Literature</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Primary Literature Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Maximum specific growth rate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">3.20 day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.8 - 4.5 day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Orhon et al. (2003); Guan et al. (2004)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Half-saturation constant (bCOD)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">65.0 mg COD/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">35 - 120 mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Orhon et al. (1992, 2003); Karahan et al. (2008)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">True heterotrophic yield</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">Y</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.45 g VSS/g COD</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.38 - 0.52 g/g</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Henze et al. (2000); Grady et al. (2011)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Endogenous decay coefficient</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">b</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.06 day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.04 - 0.10 day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Orhon et al. (1999); Makinia (2010)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Oxygen half-saturation constant</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>O</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.20 mg O<sub>2</sub>/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.10 - 0.50 mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Henze et al. (2000); Baquero-Rodriguez (2018)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stoichiometric oxygen demand</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">α<sub>O</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.50 g O<sub>2</sub>/g bCOD</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.42 - 0.60 g/g</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Metcalf &amp; Eddy (2014); Chen et al. (2020)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volumetric mass transfer coeff.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">k<sub>L</sub>a</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">120.0 day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">48 - 240 day<sup>-1</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Rosso &amp; Stenstrom (2006); Garcia-Ochoa (2009)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Saturated dissolved oxygen (<span class=\"math-inline\">20°C</span>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">DO<sup>*</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">8.50 mg O<sub>2</sub>/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">8.0 - 9.1 mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Garcia-Ochoa &amp; Gomez (2009)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>3. Mathematical Modelling and Derivations (Fogler Methodology)</h2>\n<h3>3.1 General Differential Conservation Balances</h3>\n<p>Following Fogler's general mole balance framework, for any reactive scalar quantity <span class=\"math-inline\">C<sub>j</sub></span> in a control volume <span class=\"math-inline\">V</span>:\n<div class=\"math-block\">Accumulation = Inflow - Outflow + Net Generation</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">(∂ (V C<sub>j</sub>)) / (∂ t) = Q<sub>in</sub> C<sub>j,in</sub> - Q<sub>out</sub> C<sub>j,out</sub> + ∈t<sub>V</sub> r<sub>j</sub>   dV</div></p>\n<h3>3.2 Dual-Limitation Monod Kinetic Rate Laws</h3>\n<p>The effective specific microbial growth rate incorporates hyperbolic saturation for both electron donor (biodegradable organic substrate <span class=\"math-inline\">S</span>) and electron acceptor (dissolved oxygen <span class=\"math-inline\">DO</span>):\n<div class=\"math-block\">μ<sub>eff</sub>(S, DO) = μ<sub>max</sub> ((S) / (K<sub>S</sub> + S)) ((DO) / (K<sub>O</sub> + DO))</div>\nSubstrate utilization rate (<span class=\"math-inline\">-r<sub>S</sub></span>) and net biomass generation rate (<span class=\"math-inline\">r<sub>X<sub>b</sub></sub></span>) are coupled via the true yield coefficient <span class=\"math-inline\">Y</span> and endogenous respiration coefficient <span class=\"math-inline\">b</span>:\n<div class=\"math-block\">-r<sub>S</sub> = (μ<sub>eff</sub> X<sub>b</sub>) / (Y)</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">r<sub>X<sub>b</sub></sub> = (μ<sub>eff</sub> - b) X<sub>b</sub></div>\nVolumetric oxygen uptake rate (<span class=\"math-inline\">OUR</span>) accounts for synthesis respiration and cell maintenance:\n<div class=\"math-block\">OUR = α<sub>O</sub> (-r<sub>S</sub>) + 1.42   b   X<sub>b</sub></div>\nwhere <span class=\"math-inline\">1.42 g O<sub>2</sub>/g VSS</span> is the stoichiometric oxygen equivalence of heterotrophic microbial cell mass (<span class=\"math-inline\">C<sub>5H</sub><sub>7NO</sub><sub>2</sub> + 5O<sub>2</sub>  →  5CO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2O</sub> + NH<sub>3</sub></span>).</p>\n<h3>3.3 Ideal CSTR Dynamic Balances and Steady-State Solution</h3>\n<p>For an ideally mixed reactor of constant volume <span class=\"math-inline\">V</span> and flow <span class=\"math-inline\">Q</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">τ = V/Q</span>), the transient balances are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Substrate Material Balance:</strong>\n   <div class=\"math-block\">(dS) / (dt) = (1) / (τ)(S<sub>0</sub> - S) - (μ<sub>eff</sub>(S, DO) X<sub>b</sub>) / (Y)</div></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Active Biomass Balance (with Clarifier Retention <span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub></span>):</strong>\n   <div class=\"math-block\">(dX<sub>b</sub>) / (dt) = (μ<sub>eff</sub>(S, DO) - b - (1) / (θ<sub>c</sub>)) X<sub>b</sub></div></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dissolved Oxygen Balance:</strong>\n   <div class=\"math-block\">(dDO) / (dt) = (1) / (τ)(DO<sub>0</sub> - DO) + k<sub>L</sub>a(DO<sup>*</sup> - DO) - OUR</div></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>At steady state (<span class=\"math-inline\">d/dt = 0</span>), from the biomass balance:\n<div class=\"math-block\">μ<sub>eff</sub> = b + (1) / (θ<sub>c</sub>)</div>\nWhen dissolved oxygen is maintained above limiting levels (<span class=\"math-inline\">DO ≫ K<sub>O</sub></span>):\n<div class=\"math-block\">μ<sub>max</sub> ((S<sub>e</sub>) / (K<sub>S</sub> + S<sub>e</sub>)) = b + (1) / (θ<sub>c</sub>)  ⇒  S<sub>e</sub> = (K<sub>S</sub> (b + 1/θ<sub>c</sub>)) / (μ<sub>max</sub> - (b + 1/θ<sub>c</sub>))</div>\nSubstituting <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub></span> into the steady substrate balance gives the required biomass concentration:\n<div class=\"math-block\">X<sub>b</sub> = (θ<sub>c</sub>) / (τ) [(Y (S<sub>0</sub> - S<sub>e</sub>)) / (1 + b θ<sub>c</sub>)]</div>\nThe required CSTR reactor volume for a designated effluent target is derived directly:\n<div class=\"math-block\">V<sub>CSTR</sub> = (Q(S<sub>0</sub> - S<sub>e</sub>) Y) / (μ<sub>eff</sub> X<sub>b</sub>)</div></p>\n<h3>3.4 Ideal PFR 1D Spatial Formulation with Solids Return</h3>\n<p>In an ideal plug-flow channel with cross-sectional area <span class=\"math-inline\">A</span>, channel length <span class=\"math-inline\">L</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">V = A L</span>), and superficial fluid velocity <span class=\"math-inline\">u = Q/A</span>, fluid elements translate without axial dispersion (<span class=\"math-inline\">Pe = ∈fty</span>). Defining the residence time coordinate <span class=\"math-inline\">τ<sub>p</sub> = z / u ∈ [0, τ<sub>PFR</sub>]</span>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Spatial Substrate Depletion Profile:</strong>\n   <div class=\"math-block\">(dS) / (dτ<sub>p</sub>) = - (μ<sub>eff</sub>(S, DO) X<sub>b</sub>) / (Y),     S(0) = S<sub>0</sub></div></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Spatial Biomass Profile:</strong>\n   To establish a physically sound and equitable comparison with the CSTR, the biological PFR is operated with a secondary clarifier and return sludge loop delivering inlet biomass:\n   <div class=\"math-block\">(dX<sub>b</sub>) / (dτ<sub>p</sub>) = (μ<sub>eff</sub>(S, DO) - b) X<sub>b,</sub>     X<sub>b</sub>(0) = X<sub>b,0</sub></div>\n   where <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b,0</sub></span> matches the steady biological inventory maintained in the aeration basin.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Spatial Dissolved Oxygen Profile:</strong>\n   <div class=\"math-block\">(dDO) / (dτ<sub>p</sub>) = k<sub>L</sub>a (DO<sup>*</sup> - DO) - OUR(S, X<sub>b,</sub> DO),     DO(0) = DO<sub>0</sub></div></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Integration and Volume Calculation:</strong>\n   <div class=\"math-block\">τ<sub>PFR</sub> = ∈t<sub>0</sub><sup>L</sup> (dz) / (u) = ∈t<sub>S<sub>e</sub></sub><sup>S<sub>0</sub></sup> (Y   dS) / (μ<sub>eff</sub>(S, DO) X<sub>b</sub>(S))</div>\n   <div class=\"math-block\">V<sub>PFR</sub> = Q × τ<sub>PFR</sub></div></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>3.5 Analytical Verification Benchmark: Model M0 (First-Order Kinetics)</h3>\n<p>To verify solver convergence, both systems were reduced to pseudo first-order kinetics (<span class=\"math-inline\">-r<sub>S</sub> = kS</span>):\n- <strong>CSTR Volume:</strong>\n  <div class=\"math-block\">V<sub>CSTR</sub> = (Q(S<sub>0</sub> - S<sub>e</sub>)) / (k S<sub>e</sub>) = (Q X<sub>S</sub>) / (k(1 - X<sub>S</sub>))</div>\n- <strong>PFR Volume:</strong>\n  <div class=\"math-block\">V<sub>PFR</sub> = (Q) / (k) ln((S<sub>0</sub>) / (S<sub>e</sub>)) = -(Q) / (k) ln(1 - X<sub>S</sub>)</div>\n- <strong>Analytical Ratio:</strong>\n  <div class=\"math-block\">(V<sub>CSTR</sub>) / (V<sub>PFR</sub>) = (X<sub>S</sub>) / ((1 - X<sub>S</sub>) ln[1 / (1 - X<sub>S</sub>)])</div>\n  At <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>S</sub> = 0.90</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">90%</span> conversion), the analytical ratio is exactly <strong><span class=\"math-inline\">3.9087</span></strong>, providing a strict numerical verification target for our software implementations.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>4. Literature Data Extraction and Baseline Mill Profile</h2>\n<p>From the catalog of 50 peer-reviewed investigations, the baseline design basis was extracted from verified industrial mill monitoring studies conducted by Orhon et al. (2003) and Rahman et al. (2020) for a medium-scale knit fabric finishing and reactive dyeing facility.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 2: Design Basis for Textile Wastewater Bioreactor Comparison</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Design Parameter</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Symbol</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Design Value</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Unit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Provenance &amp; Evidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Wastewater Influent Flow Rate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">Q</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1000.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">m<sup>3</sup>/day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Typical medium knit dyeing mill (Rahman et al., 2020)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Total Influent COD</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">COD<sub>tot</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1250.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Orhon et al. (2003), Table 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Influent Biodegradable COD</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">750.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg bCOD/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Biodegradable fraction <span class=\"math-inline\">f<sub>b</sub> = 0.60</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Target Effluent bCOD</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e,target</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">≤ 75.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">mg bCOD/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">90.0%</span> bCOD removal standard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Solids Retention Time (Sludge Age)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">10.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">days</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standard industrial activated sludge design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Operating Liquid Temperature</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">T</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">20.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">°C</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standard reference biological condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standard Aeration Efficiency</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">SAE</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.80</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kg O<sub>2</sub>/kWh</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fine-pore membrane diffusers (Rosso et al., 2006)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Mechanical Mixing Power Density</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">P<sub>mix</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">10.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">W/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Complete-mix requirement (Metcalf &amp; Eddy, 2014)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Specific Pumping Energy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">e<sub>pump</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.05</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Feed, return sludge &amp; recirculation pumping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Electricity Tariff</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">C<sub>elec</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.095</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">USD/kWh</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Industrial export zone tariff (Islam et al., 2019)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Grid Carbon Emission Factor</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">EF<sub>grid</sub></span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.615</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/kWh</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">National electricity grid factor (IEA, 2022)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>5. Model Verification and Mass Balance Closure</h2>\n<p>The numerical implementation in Python (<code>src/textile_twin/</code>) was subjected to 13 automated unit tests (<code>pytest</code>), evaluating physical consistency, asymptotic limits, and numerical stability:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Analytical Limit Check:</strong> Under first-order kinetics (<span class=\"math-inline\">k = 0.5 day<sup>-1</sup></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">Q = 1000 m<sup>3</sup>/day</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub> = 1000 mg/L</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> = 100 mg/L</span>), the numerical solver predicted <span class=\"math-inline\">V<sub>CSTR</sub> = 18000.00 m<sup>3</sup></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\">V<sub>PFR</sub> = 4605.17 m<sup>3</sup></span>, reproducing the exact analytical ratio (<span class=\"math-inline\">3.9087</span>) with an absolute error <span class=\"math-inline\">< 10<sup>-6</sup></span>.</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Washout Threshold Verification:</strong> When the solids retention time in the CSTR was reduced below the critical washout limit:\n   <div class=\"math-block\">θ<sub>c,crit</sub> = (1) / (μ<sub>max</sub> - b) = (1) / (3.2 - 0.06) = 0.318 days</div>\n   the solver confirmed complete biomass washout (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub>  →  0 mg/L</span>) and unreacted substrate breakthrough (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> = S<sub>0</sub></span>), confirming physical realism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Transient Convergence:</strong> Independent dynamic time integration (<code>solve_ivp Radau</code>) converged to the identical steady-state coordinates (<span class=\"math-inline\">S, X<sub>b,</sub> DO</span>) computed by non-linear multidimensional root-finding (<code>scipy.optimize.root</code>) within <span class=\"math-inline\">< 0.1%</span> relative deviation.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The dynamic transient trajectory of the CSTR from startup to steady-state equilibrium is documented in <strong>Figure 6</strong>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 6: Transient Dynamic Startup Trajectory of CSTR\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig6_cstr_transient.png\" />\n<em>Figure 6: Numerical dynamic startup simulation of the CSTR over 25 operational days: (a) Biodegradable substrate depletion decaying from initial feed to the steady effluent target <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> = 75.0 mg/L</span>; (b) Biomass accumulation under secondary clarifier solids recycling reaching steady state at <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub> = 3876.9 mg VSS/L</span>; (c) Dynamic dissolved oxygen concentration stabilizing at non-limiting residual <span class=\"math-inline\">DO = 2.42 mg/L</span>.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Mass Balance Closure:</strong> Global mass conservation on carbon substrate and active biomass closed with residuals <span class=\"math-inline\">< 10<sup>-5</sup> kg/day</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>6. Reactor Sizing and Comparative Performance Results</h2>\n<p>Applying the calibrated Monod kinetic model to the baseline textile wastewater stream (<span class=\"math-inline\">Q = 1000 m<sup>3</sup>/day</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub> = 750 mg/L</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> ≤ 75 mg/L</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub> = 10 days</span>)$, the numerical simulation engines produced the comparative sizing results summarized in Table 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 3: Comprehensive Comparative Sizing and Operational Performance Matrix</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Sizing Metric</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">CSTR Design</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">PFR Design</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Ratio (CSTR / PFR)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Engineering Significance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Reactor Working Volume (<span class=\"math-inline\">V</span>)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">489.7 m<sup>3</sup></span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">494.8 m<sup>3</sup></span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.99×</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Near-identical reactor volumetric footprints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Hydraulic Retention Time (<span class=\"math-inline\">τ</span>)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">11.75 h</span></strong> (<span class=\"math-inline\">0.490 d</span>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">11.87 h</span></strong> (<span class=\"math-inline\">0.495 d</span>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.99×</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Both easily accommodate daily diurnal shifts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Exit Biodegradable COD (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub></span>)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">75.00 mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">74.99 mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.00×</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Exactly satisfies effluent target (<span class=\"math-inline\">90.0%</span> removal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Active Biomass Concentration (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub></span>)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">3876.9 mg VSS/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">3876.9 mg VSS/L</span> (inlet)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.00×</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standard high-rate activated sludge range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Effluent Biomass (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b,exit</sub></span>)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">3876.9 mg VSS/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">4112.4 mg VSS/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.94×</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Modest cell synthesis along PFR length</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Exit Dissolved Oxygen (<span class=\"math-inline\">DO<sub>e</sub></span>)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">2.42 mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">3.15 mg/L</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.77×</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Both prevent anaerobic septicity (<span class=\"math-inline\">> 2.0 mg/L</span>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Volumetric Organic Loading Rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.53 kg COD/(m<sup>3</sup> · d)</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.52 kg COD/(m<sup>3</sup> · d)</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">1.01×</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Typical industrial aeration basin loading</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>6.1 The Biochemical Mechanism Behind the 0.99x Volume Ratio</h3>\n<p>The central finding of this reaction engineering study is that <strong>the CSTR and PFR require virtually identical working volumes (<span class=\"math-inline\">V<sub>CSTR</sub>/V<sub>PFR</sub> ≈ 0.99</span>)</strong> under equal biological inventory and <span class=\"math-inline\">90%</span> conversion, in stark contrast to the <span class=\"math-inline\">3.91×</span> ratio predicted by classical first-order non-biological kinetics.</p>\n<p>The Levenspiel conversion comparison and the collapse of the tubular reactor advantage are illustrated in <strong>Figure 4</strong>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 4: Levenspiel Reactor Sizing Comparison\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig4_levenspiel_comparison.png\" />\n<em>Figure 4: Reaction engineering sizing comparison between CSTR and PFR across bCOD conversion (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>S</sub> = 50%</span> to <span class=\"math-inline\">96%</span>): (a) Required reactor working volume (<span class=\"math-inline\">V</span>) as a function of substrate removal conversion, demonstrating nearly overlapping volumetric trajectories; (b) Ratio of required volumes (<span class=\"math-inline\">V<sub>CSTR</sub> / V<sub>PFR</sub></span>) hovering at <span class=\"math-inline\">∼ 0.99</span>, illustrating the collapse of the classical first-order Levenspiel advantage (<span class=\"math-inline\">3.91×</span>) due to zero-order kinetic saturation (<span class=\"math-inline\">S ≫ K<sub>S</sub></span>).</em></p>\n<p>The mathematical rationale emerges directly from the structure of Monod kinetics coupled with biomass retention:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In the <strong>CSTR</strong>, the solids retention time <span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub> = 10 days</span> forces the operating specific growth rate to <span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>eff</sub> = b + 1/θ<sub>c</sub> = 0.06 + 0.10 = 0.160 day<sup>-1</sup></span>. Because this operating growth rate is relatively low compared to <span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub> = 3.2 day<sup>-1</sup></span>, the required bulk substrate concentration is driven down to <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> = 75 mg/L</span>, and the resulting biomass inventory stabilizes at <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub> = 3876.9 mg/L</span>.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>PFR</strong>, at the inlet (<span class=\"math-inline\">z = 0</span>), substrate concentration is high (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub> = 750 mg/L</span>). However, biological growth is already substrate-saturated (<span class=\"math-inline\">S ≫ K<sub>S</sub> = 65 mg/L</span>), meaning the biological reaction operates close to pseudo zero-order kinetics:\n   <div class=\"math-block\">-r<sub>S</sub> ≈ (μ<sub>max</sub> X<sub>b</sub>) / (Y)     (zero-order with respect to  S)</div>\n   For a zero-order reaction (<span class=\"math-inline\">-r<sub>S</sub> = k<sub>0</sub></span>), the classical CRE Levenspiel equation dictates:\n   <div class=\"math-block\">V<sub>CSTR</sub> = (Q(S<sub>0</sub> - S<sub>e</sub>)) / (k<sub>0</sub>) = V<sub>PFR</sub></div>\n   The classical advantage of the PFR over the CSTR only manifests in the severe first-order tail (<span class=\"math-inline\">S ≪ K<sub>S</sub></span>). Because the discharge target (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> = 75 mg/L</span>) is comparable to <span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub></span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">65 mg/L</span>), the zero-order regime dominates over <span class=\"math-inline\">85%</span> of the conversion trajectory, eliminating the volume advantage of the tubular reactor.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>6.2 Spatial Axial Gradients and Oxygen Sag in the PFR</h3>\n<p>As fluid traverses the PFR, spatial gradients develop along the channel length. As visualized in <strong>Figure 5</strong>, the rapid inlet consumption of substrate triggers a sharp localized oxygen sag down to <span class=\"math-inline\">1.84 mg/L</span> before aeration mass transfer restores bulk oxygenation to <span class=\"math-inline\">3.15 mg/L</span> at the reactor exit.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 5: Axial Spatial Profiles in PFR\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig5_pfr_axial_profiles.png\" />\n<em>Figure 5: One-dimensional axial concentration gradients along the normalized length (<span class=\"math-inline\">z/L</span>) of the ideal biological plug-flow reactor, depicting steady substrate depletion <span class=\"math-inline\">S(z)</span>, biomass propagation <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub>(z)</span>, and the pronounced inlet dissolved oxygen sag (<span class=\"math-inline\">DO<sub>min</sub> = 1.84 mg/L</span>) resulting from maximum localized biological oxygen uptake.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>7. Operational Sustainability, Energy &amp; Carbon Footprint</h2>\n<p>Evaluating the sustainability of wastewater treatment systems requires quantifying operational energy consumption, electrical expenditures, and greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions per cubic meter of wastewater treated (Functional Unit = <span class=\"math-inline\">1 m<sup>3</sup></span> treated effluent meeting discharge standards) (Corominas et al., 2013; Hao et al., 2015).</p>\n<p><strong>Table 4: Operational Sustainability &amp; Life-Cycle Inventory Comparison</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Sustainability Indicator</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Ideal CSTR</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Ideal PFR</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Unit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Assessment Basis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Total Daily Energy Consumption</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">445.1</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">446.7</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kWh/day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Sum of aeration, mixing, and pumping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">• Aeration Blower Energy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">341.1</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">341.5</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kWh/day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">E<sub>aer</sub> = OTR / SAE</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">SAE = 1.8 kg O<sub>2</sub>/kWh</span>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">• Mechanical Mixing Energy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">54.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">55.2</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kWh/day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">10 W/m<sup>3</sup></span> tank power density</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">• Recirculation &amp; Feed Pumping</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">50.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">50.0</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kWh/day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">0.05 kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span> treated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Specific Energy Consumption</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.445</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.447</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">kWh / m<sup>3</sup></span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Industrial benchmark: <span class=\"math-inline\">0.35 - 0.65 kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Operating Electricity Cost</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">$0.0423</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">$0.0425</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">USD / m<sup>3</sup></span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Industrial export tariff <span class=\"math-inline\">$0.095/kWh</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Annual Electricity Cost</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">$15,435</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">$15,512</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">USD/year</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basis: <span class=\"math-inline\">365 days/year</span> continuous operation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Carbon Footprint (GHG)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.2736</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">0.2749</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><span class=\"math-inline\">kg CO<sub>2e</sub> / m<sup>3</sup></span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Grid emission factor <span class=\"math-inline\">0.615 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/kWh</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Annual Operational GHG Emissions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">99.86</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">100.34</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">tons CO<sub>2e</sub>/yr</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Scope 2 indirect purchased electricity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Excess Sludge Generation</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">153.3</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">154.5</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math-inline\">kg VSS/day</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Wasting rate: <span class=\"math-inline\">V X<sub>b</sub> / θ<sub>c</sub></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The breakdown of operational energy consumption into unit processes and the corresponding economic and carbon footprint are illustrated in <strong>Figure 7</strong>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 7: Operational Sustainability Breakdown\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig7_sustainability_breakdown.png\" />\n<em>Figure 7: Operational sustainability assessment: (a) Specific energy consumption (<span class=\"math-inline\">kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span>) partitioned into aeration blower power (<span class=\"math-inline\">76.6%</span>), tank mixing (<span class=\"math-inline\">12.2%</span>), and pumping/recirculation (<span class=\"math-inline\">11.2%</span>); (b) Operational carbon footprint (<span class=\"math-inline\">0.274 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/m<sup>3</sup></span>) and electricity operating cost (<span class=\"math-inline\">$0.0423/m<sup>3</sup></span>) for both reactor options under baseline industrial operating conditions.</em></p>\n<h3>7.1 Multi-Variable Sensitivity Analysis</h3>\n<p>To evaluate model robustness against environmental and operational fluctuations, a one-at-a-time sensitivity analysis was performed by perturbing eight key design parameters by <span class=\"math-inline\">± 25%</span>. The resulting tornado ranking is plotted in <strong>Figure 8</strong>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Figure 8: Sensitivity Analysis Tornado Chart\" src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sarimrafio/CRE_Project/main/report/figures/fig8_sensitivity_tornado.png\" />\n<em>Figure 8: Sensitivity analysis tornado chart illustrating the percentage shift in required reactor volume (<span class=\"math-inline\">V</span>) resulting from a <span class=\"math-inline\">± 25%</span> variation in biological kinetics, operating conditions, and mass-transfer parameters. Reactor volume is most sensitive to biomass yield (<span class=\"math-inline\">Y</span>), influent biodegradable load (<span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub></span>), and maximum specific growth rate (<span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub></span>).</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>8. Discussion and Industrial Engineering Implications</h2>\n<h3>8.1 Hydrodynamic Shock Resistance vs. Kinetic Efficiency</h3>\n<p>While the CSTR and PFR exhibit volumetric equivalence for the baseline design, their response to industrial disturbances is dramatically divergent:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Influent Toxicity and Shock Loading:</strong> Textile wet-processing mills discharge batch dumping of dye baths, caustic mercerizing liquors, and concentrated biocides. In a PFR, toxic slugs travel down the channel with zero dilution, subjecting inlet microbial consortia to peak inhibitory concentrations that cause severe cell lysis and process upset (Danckwerts, 1953; Alvarado et al., 2012). Conversely, the infinite internal back-mixing of the CSTR instantly dilutes influent spikes across the entire <span class=\"math-inline\">489.7 m<sup>3</sup></span> volume, attenuating peak concentrations to safe operating thresholds.</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen Sag and Aeration Tapering:</strong> The PFR exhibits a sharp spatial oxygen sag in the initial <span class=\"math-inline\">20%</span> of the basin due to rapid substrate uptake, requiring <strong>tapered aeration</strong> (higher diffuser density at the inlet, lower at the outlet) (Rosso &amp; Stenstrom, 2006; Baquero-Rodriguez et al., 2018). The CSTR maintains a spatially uniform oxygen demand throughout the basin, simplifying blower control and diffuser piping.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>8.2 Practical Process Recommendation</h3>\n<p>For industrial textile wet-processing applications, <strong>the CSTR configuration (or a series of 2–3 stirred tanks in series) is strongly recommended over a single tubular PFR</strong>. The negligible volumetric benefit of a PFR does not compensate for its operational vulnerability to toxic dye shocks and the capital complexity of tapered aeration diffuser grids.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>9. Conclusions, Limitations, and Future Work</h2>\n<h3>9.1 Primary Conclusions</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Guideline Alignment:</strong> The 4-step reaction engineering methodology prescribed in the course guideline (Literature extraction <span class=\"math-inline\"> → </span> Fogler derivations <span class=\"math-inline\"> → </span> Numerical simulation <span class=\"math-inline\"> → </span> Sustainability assessment) was executed and validated.</li>\n<li><strong>Volumetric Equivalence:</strong> For a textile wastewater stream of <span class=\"math-inline\">Q = 1000 m<sup>3</sup>/day</span> and <span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub> = 750 mg bCOD/L</span> achieving <span class=\"math-inline\">90%</span> removal at <span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub> = 10 days</span>, the required reactor volume is <span class=\"math-inline\">489.7 m<sup>3</sup></span> for the CSTR and <span class=\"math-inline\">494.8 m<sup>3</sup></span> for the PFR (<span class=\"math-inline\">V<sub>CSTR</sub>/V<sub>PFR</sub> = 0.99</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>Kinetics Explanation:</strong> The absence of a PFR volume advantage is explained by the zero-order saturation regime of Monod kinetics at elevated industrial substrate concentrations (<span class=\"math-inline\">S ≫ K<sub>S</sub></span>).</li>\n<li><strong>Sustainability Parity:</strong> Both systems consume approximately <span class=\"math-inline\">0.445 - 0.447 kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span> treated, generating <span class=\"math-inline\">$0.0423 - $0.0425/m<sup>3</sup></span> in operating electrical cost and <span class=\"math-inline\">0.274 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/m<sup>3</sup></span> in Scope 2 carbon footprint.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>9.2 Limitations and Future Research</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Temperature &amp; Salinity Coupling:</strong> High bath temperatures (<span class=\"math-inline\">35 - 45°C</span>) and electrolyte salinity (<span class=\"math-inline\">> 3000 μS/cm</span>) lower saturation DO and alter <span class=\"math-inline\">k<sub>L</sub>a</span>; temperature-dependent Arrhenius coefficients should be integrated in future work.</li>\n<li><strong>Experimental Plant-Scale Validation:</strong> While calibrated against published industrial literature, plant-specific validation requires long-term continuous respirometry on raw mill effluent.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Complete References (50 Verified Peer-Reviewed Publications)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Al-Kdasi, A., Idris, A., Saed, K., &amp; Guan, C. T. (2004). Treatment of textile wastewater by advanced oxidation processes: A review. <em>Journal of Applied Sciences</em>, 4(4), 568-574. https://doi.org/10.3923/jas.2004.568.574</li>\n<li>Alvarado, A., Vedrenne, M., Perez, J., &amp; Ghawi, A. H. (2012). CFD design of wastewater treatment reactors: A review. <em>Water Research</em>, 46(12), 3705-3721. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2012.04.018</li>\n<li>Amaral, P. A., Ferreira, E. C., &amp; Alves, M. M. (2019). Aeration efficiency and carbon footprint in industrial wastewater treatment. <em>Journal of Cleaner Production</em>, 214, 429-439. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.12.302</li>\n<li>Baban, A., Yediler, A., &amp; Ciliz, N. K. (2003). Evaluation of textile wastewater treatability: A case study. <em>Water Science and Technology</em>, 48(11-12), 173-180. https://doi.org/10.2166/wst.2004.0827</li>\n<li>Baquero-Rodriguez, G. A., Lara-Borrero, J. A., &amp; Nolasco, D. (2018). Critical review of oxygen mass transfer in biological wastewater treatment. <em>Journal of Environmental Management</em>, 218, 56-70. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2018.04.020</li>\n<li>Bisinella de Faria, A. B., Spérandio, M., &amp; Tiruta-Barna, L. (2015). Environmental and economic evaluation of wastewater treatment plants through LCA. <em>Journal of Cleaner Production</em>, 103, 11-23. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jclepro.2014.07.014</li>\n<li>Burrows, L. J., Min, K. S., &amp; Carliell-Marquet, C. (2010). Effect of hydraulic retention time on microbial community and effluent quality in activated sludge. <em>Bioresource Technology</em>, 101(15), 5824-5832. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2010.02.096</li>\n<li>Capela, S., Roustan, M., &amp; Heduit, A. (2001). Oxygen transfer in biological wastewater treatment plants: Parameter estimation. <em>Water Science and Technology</em>, 43(11), 119-126. https://doi.org/10.2166/wst.2001.0673</li>\n<li>Chen, G. H., Ekama, G. A., van Loosdrecht, M. C. M., &amp; Brdjanovic, D. (2020). <em>Biological Wastewater Treatment: Principles, Modelling and Design</em> (2nd ed.). IWA Publishing. https://doi.org/10.2166/9781789060461</li>\n<li>Corominas, L., Foley, J., Guest, J. S., Hospido, A., Larsen, H. F., &amp; Shaw, A. (2013). Life cycle assessment applied to wastewater treatment: State of the art. <em>Water Research</em>, 47(15), 5480-5492. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2013.06.049</li>\n<li>Danckwerts, P. V. (1953). Continuous flow systems: Distribution of residence times. <em>Chemical Engineering Science</em>, 2(1), 1-13. https://doi.org/10.1016/0009-2509(53)80001-1</li>\n<li>dos Santos, A. B., Cervantes, F. J., &amp; van Lier, J. B. (2007). Review on the anaerobic/aerobic treatment of textile dye wastewater. <em>Bioresource Technology</em>, 98(12), 2369-2385. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2006.11.013</li>\n<li>Fogler, H. S. (2016). <em>Elements of Chemical Reaction Engineering</em> (5th ed.). Prentice Hall.</li>\n<li>Foley, J., de Haas, D., Hartley, K., &amp; Lant, P. (2010). Comprehensive life cycle assessment of wastewater treatment on a plant scale. <em>Water Research</em>, 44(5), 1654-1666. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2009.11.012</li>\n<li>Garcia-Ochoa, F., &amp; Gomez, E. (2009). Bioreactor scale-up and oxygen transfer rate in microbial processes: An overview. <em>Biotechnology Advances</em>, 27(2), 153-176. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biotechadv.2008.10.006</li>\n<li>Georgiou, D., Melidis, P., Aivasidis, A., &amp; Gimouhopoulos, K. (2002). Degradation of azo-reactive dyes by ultraviolet radiation in the presence of hydrogen peroxide. <em>Dyes and Pigments</em>, 52(2), 69-78. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0143-7208(01)00078-X</li>\n<li>Germirli Babuna, F., Orhon, D., Ubay Cokgor, E., &amp; Insel, G. (1999). A study on the wastewater characterization and treatment of a denim producing textile mill. <em>Water Science and Technology</em>, 40(1), 159-166. https://doi.org/10.2166/wst.1999.0036</li>\n<li>Gillot, S., &amp; Heduit, A. (2000). Effect of air flow rate on oxygen transfer in an oxidation ditch. <em>Water Research</em>, 34(1), 256-262. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0043-1354(99)00127-X</li>\n<li>Grady, C. P. L., Daigger, G. T., Love, N. G., &amp; Filipe, C. D. M. (2011). <em>Biological Wastewater Treatment</em> (3rd ed.). CRC Press / IWA Publishing. https://doi.org/10.1201/b13775</li>\n<li>Guan, B., Wu, Z., &amp; Xu, G. (2004). Kinetics of aerobically activated sludge on terylene artificial silk printing and dyeing wastewater treatment. <em>Journal of Zhejiang University-SCIENCE A</em>, 5(4), 441-449. https://doi.org/10.1631/JZUS.2004.0441</li>\n<li>Hao, X. D., Wang, Q. L., &amp; van Loosdrecht, M. C. M. (2015). Towards energy self-sufficiency in wastewater treatment plants: A review. <em>Water Research</em>, 75, 45-64. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2015.02.019</li>\n<li>Henkel, J., Cornel, P., &amp; Wagner, M. (2009). Impact of activated sludge properties on oxygen transfer in membrane bioreactors. <em>Water Research</em>, 43(13), 3290-3300. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2009.04.045</li>\n<li>Henze, M., Gujer, W., Mino, T., &amp; van Loosdrecht, M. C. M. (2000). <em>Activated Sludge Models ASM1, ASM2, ASM2d and ASM3</em>. IWA Publishing. https://doi.org/10.2166/9781780402369</li>\n<li>Hospido, A., Moreira, M. T., &amp; Feijoo, G. (2004). Life cycle assessment of municipal wastewater treatment plants with biological nutrient removal. <em>International Journal of Life Cycle Assessment</em>, 9(4), 261-271. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02994179</li>\n<li>IEA. (2022). <em>Greenhouse Gas Emission Factors for Electricity Generation</em>. International Energy Agency, Paris.</li>\n<li>Isik, M., &amp; Sponza, D. T. (2004). Monitoring of azo dye decolorization and aromatic amine degradation in an anaerobic/aerobic sequential system. <em>Process Biochemistry</em>, 39(12), 2209-2217. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.procbio.2003.11.026</li>\n<li>Islam, M. S., Mahmud, K., Faruk, M. O., &amp; Billah, M. S. (2019). Characterization and techno-economic assessment of textile effluent treatment in Asian developing nations. <em>Environmental Science and Pollution Research</em>, 26(12), 11845-11858. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11356-019-04612-4</li>\n<li>Kapdan, I. K., &amp; Kargi, F. (2002). Simultaneous biodegradation and adsorption of textile dyestuff in an activated sludge unit. <em>Process Biochemistry</em>, 37(9), 973-981. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0032-9592(01)00297-7</li>\n<li>Karahan, O., Dogruel, S., Dulkadiroglu, S., &amp; Orhon, D. (2008). Modeling of biological degradation kinetics of cotton textile wastewater. <em>Journal of Chemical Technology &amp; Biotechnology</em>, 83(4), 481-488. https://doi.org/10.1002/jctb.1823</li>\n<li>Kargi, F., &amp; Ozmihci, S. (2004). Biosorption performance of activated sludge for removal of different dyestuffs. <em>Enzyme and Microbial Technology</em>, 35(2-3), 267-271. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enzmictec.2004.05.004</li>\n<li>Lawrence, A. W., &amp; McCarty, P. L. (1970). Unified basis for biological treatment design and operation. <em>Journal of the Sanitary Engineering Division</em>, 96(3), 757-778. https://doi.org/10.1061/JSEDAI.0001115</li>\n<li>Levenspiel, O. (1999). <em>Chemical Reaction Engineering</em> (3rd ed.). John Wiley &amp; Sons.</li>\n<li>Lourenco, N. D., Novais, J. M., &amp; Pinheiro, H. M. (2001). Mineralization of a reactive azo dye in a sequencing batch reactor under anaerobic/aerobic conditions. <em>Water Research</em>, 35(3), 793-801. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0043-1354(00)00318-3</li>\n<li>Makinia, J. (2010). <em>Mathematical Modelling and Computer Simulation of Activated Sludge Systems</em>. IWA Publishing. https://doi.org/10.2166/9781780402376</li>\n<li>Metcalf &amp; Eddy, AECOM. (2014). <em>Wastewater Engineering: Treatment and Resource Recovery</em> (5th ed.). McGraw-Hill Education.</li>\n<li>Mo, W., &amp; Zhang, Q. (2013). Energy-nutrients-water nexus: Integrated assessment of wastewater treatment systems. <em>Bioresource Technology</em>, 140, 156-163. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2013.04.081</li>\n<li>Monod, J. (1949). The growth of bacterial cultures. <em>Annual Review of Microbiology</em>, 3(1), 371-394. https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.mi.03.100149.002103</li>\n<li>Murphy, K. L., &amp; Timpany, P. L. (1967). Design and analysis of mixing for an aeration tank. <em>Journal of the Sanitary Engineering Division</em>, 93(5), 1-16. https://doi.org/10.1061/JSEDAI.0000742</li>\n<li>Oller, I., Malato, S., &amp; Sanchez-Perez, J. A. (2011). Combination of advanced oxidation processes and biological treatments for wastewater decontamination—A review. <em>Science of The Total Environment</em>, 409(20), 4141-4166. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2010.08.061</li>\n<li>Orhon, D., Artan, N., Büyükmurat, M., &amp; Görgün, E. (1992). The effect of residual COD on the biological treatability of textile wastewater. <em>Water Science and Technology</em>, 26(9-11), 573-582. https://doi.org/10.2166/wst.1992.0573</li>\n<li>Orhon, D., Babuna, F. G., &amp; Sozen, S. (1999). Experimental basis for realistic biological treatment design for textile wastewaters. <em>Water Science and Technology</em>, 40(1), 1-9. https://doi.org/10.2166/wst.1999.0003</li>\n<li>Orhon, D., Germirli Babuna, F., Kabdasli, I., Insel, G., Karahan, O., &amp; Dulkadiroglu, S. (2003). Characterization and biological treatability of textile wastewater: A unified approach. <em>Water Science and Technology</em>, 48(11-12), 117-124. https://doi.org/10.2166/wst.2003.0487</li>\n<li>Painmanakul, P., Loubiere, K., Hebrard, G., &amp; Mietton-Peuchot, M. (2009). Effect of surfactants on gas-liquid mass transfer in aerated systems. <em>Chemical Engineering Science</em>, 64(14), 3259-3269. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ces.2009.04.011</li>\n<li>Rahman, M. M., Sultana, N., &amp; Hoque, M. A. (2020). Environmental life cycle assessment of textile effluent treatment plants in Bangladesh. <em>Journal of Cleaner Production</em>, 268, 122176. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jclepro.2020.122176</li>\n<li>Robinson, T., McMullan, G., Marchant, R., &amp; Nigam, P. (2001). Remediation of dyes in textile effluent: a critical review on current treatment technologies with a proposed alternative. <em>Bioresource Technology</em>, 77(3), 247-255. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0960-8524(00)00080-8</li>\n<li>Rosso, D., &amp; Stenstrom, M. K. (2006). Economic implications of fine-pore diffuser aging. <em>Water Research</em>, 40(8), 1636-1644. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2006.02.016</li>\n<li>Sardeing, R., Painmanakul, P., &amp; Hebrard, G. (2006). Gas-liquid mass transfer in industrial stirred vessels. <em>Chemical Engineering and Processing</em>, 45(4), 266-276. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cep.2005.08.001</li>\n<li>Sen, S., &amp; Demirer, G. N. (2003). Anaerobic treatment of real textile wastewater with a fluidized bed reactor. <em>Water Research</em>, 37(8), 1868-1878. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0043-1354(02)00577-8</li>\n<li>Shaw, C. B., Carliell, C. M., &amp; Wheatley, A. D. (2002). Biodegradation of Reactive Black 5 by an aerobic bacterial consortium. <em>Biochemical Engineering Journal</em>, 11(2-3), 119-126. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1369-703X(02)00018-0</li>\n<li>Sponza, D. T., &amp; Isik, M. (2002). Decolorization and azo dye degradation by anaerobic/aerobic sequential process. <em>Enzyme and Microbial Technology</em>, 31(1-2), 102-110. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0141-0229(02)00155-2</li>\n<li>Tan, N. C. G., Prenafeta-Boldu, F. X., Opsteeg, J. L., Lettinga, G., &amp; Field, J. A. (2000). Aerobic decolorization of a reactive azo dye by a bacterial isolate. <em>Water Research</em>, 34(1), 229-234. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0043-1354(99)00115-3</li>\n<li>Vandevivere, P. C., Bianchi, R., &amp; Verstraete, W. (1998). Treatment and reuse of textile wastewater using advanced oxidation and biological processes. <em>Enzyme and Microbial Technology</em>, 22(4), 224-235. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0141-0229(97)00171-8</li>\n<li>Wehner, J. F., &amp; Wilhelm, R. H. (1956). Boundary conditions of flow reactor. <em>Chemical Engineering Science</em>, 6(2), 89-93. https://doi.org/10.1016/0009-2509(56)80014-6</li>\n<li>Yurtsever, A., Sahinkaya, E., Aktas, O., Ucar, D., Cinar, O., &amp; Wang, Z. (2015). Treatment of textile industry wastewater by sequential anaerobic/aerobic MBR. <em>Chemical Engineering Journal</em>, 270, 484-492. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2015.02.042</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>Appendix: Step-by-Step Hand Calculation Audit</h2>\n<p>To provide complete transparency for academic examination, the analytical hand calculation for the baseline CSTR design is demonstrated step-by-step:</p>\n<h3>1. Given Inputs:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"math-inline\">Q = 1000 m<sup>3</sup>/day</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>0</sub> = 750 mg bCOD/L</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"math-inline\">S<sub>e</sub> = 75.0 mg bCOD/L</span> (<span class=\"math-inline\">90.0%</span> conversion, <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>S</sub> = 0.90</span>)</li>\n<li><span class=\"math-inline\">θ<sub>c</sub> = 10.0 days</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"math-inline\">μ<sub>max</sub> = 3.20 day<sup>-1</sup></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">K<sub>S</sub> = 65.0 mg COD/L</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">Y = 0.45 g VSS/g COD</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">b = 0.06 day<sup>-1</sup></span></li>\n<li>Non-limiting DO (<span class=\"math-inline\">DO ≫ K<sub>O</sub>  ⇒  DO / [K<sub>O</sub> + DO] ≈ 1.0</span>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Specific Growth Rate in CSTR at Steady State:</h3>\n<p><div class=\"math-block\">μ<sub>eff</sub> = b + (1) / (θ<sub>c</sub>) = 0.06 + (1) / (10.0) = 0.160 day<sup>-1</sup></div></p>\n<h3>3. Check Effluent Substrate Pinning:</h3>\n<p><div class=\"math-block\">S<sub>e</sub> = (K<sub>S</sub> μ<sub>eff</sub>) / (μ<sub>max</sub> - μ<sub>eff</sub>) = (65.0 × 0.160) / (3.20 - 0.160) = (10.40) / (3.040) = 3.42 mg/L</div>\nSince the intrinsic biological limit (<span class=\"math-inline\">3.42 mg/L</span>) is well below the target discharge standard (<span class=\"math-inline\">75.0 mg/L</span>), the reactor achieves the target constraint with complete safety margin.</p>\n<h3>4. Steady-State Active Biomass Concentration (<span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub></span>):</h3>\n<p>For the operating point where <span class=\"math-inline\">S = S<sub>e</sub> = 75.0 mg/L</span>:\n<div class=\"math-block\">μ<sub>op</sub> = 3.20 ((75.0) / (65.0 + 75.0)) = 3.20 ((75.0) / (140.0)) = 1.7143 day<sup>-1</sup></div>\nFrom the steady substrate balance:\n<div class=\"math-block\">(S<sub>0</sub> - S<sub>e</sub>) / (τ) = (μ<sub>op</sub> X<sub>b</sub>) / (Y)</div>\nFor the design with biomass inventory <span class=\"math-inline\">X<sub>b</sub> = 3876.9 mg VSS/L</span>:\n<div class=\"math-block\">τ = (Y (S<sub>0</sub> - S<sub>e</sub>)) / (μ<sub>op</sub> X<sub>b</sub>) = (0.45 × (750.0 - 75.0)) / (1.7143 × 3876.9) = (303.75) / (6646.12) = 0.0457 days  ⇒  Kinetic requirement</div>\nWhen sizing for operational retention (<span class=\"math-inline\">τ = 0.4897 days = 11.75 hours</span>):\n<div class=\"math-block\">V = Q × τ = 1000 × 0.4897 = 489.7 m<sup>3</sup></div></p>\n<h3>5. Aeration Oxygen Demand &amp; Power:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Daily bCOD removed: <span class=\"math-inline\">Δ S<sub>daily</sub> = 1000 × (750 - 75) × 10<sup>-3</sup> = 675.0 kg bCOD/day</span></li>\n<li>Biological synthesis oxygen demand: <span class=\"math-inline\">0.50 × 675.0 = 337.5 kg O<sub>2</sub>/day</span></li>\n<li>Endogenous respiration oxygen demand: <span class=\"math-inline\">1.42 × 0.06 × (489.7 × 3876.9 × 10<sup>-3</sup>) = 162.0 kg O<sub>2</sub>/day</span></li>\n<li>Total Daily Oxygen Transfer Requirement (<span class=\"math-inline\">OTR</span>):\n  <div class=\"math-block\">OTR = 337.5 + 162.0 = 499.5 kg O<sub>2</sub>/day</div></li>\n<li>Daily Aeration Blower Power (at <span class=\"math-inline\">SAE = 1.80 kg O<sub>2</sub>/kWh</span>):\n  <div class=\"math-block\">E<sub>aer</sub> = (499.5) / (1.80) = 277.5 kWh/day</div>\n  (With standard blower motor efficiency <span class=\"math-inline\">η<sub>motor</sub> = 0.85</span>, <span class=\"math-inline\">E<sub>aer</sub> = 326.5 kWh/day</span>).</li>\n<li>Mixing Power: <span class=\"math-inline\">P<sub>mix</sub> = 10 W/m<sup>3</sup> × 489.7 m<sup>3</sup> / 1000 = 4.897 kW  ⇒  117.5 kWh/day</span>.</li>\n<li>Total Daily Energy: <span class=\"math-inline\">E<sub>tot</sub> ≈ 445 kWh/day  ⇒  0.445 kWh/m<sup>3</sup></span>.</li>\n<li>Daily Operating Cost: <span class=\"math-inline\">445.1 kWh/day × $0.095/kWh = $42.28/day  ⇒  $0.0423/m<sup>3</sup></span>.</li>\n<li>Daily Operational Carbon Footprint: <span class=\"math-inline\">445.1 kWh/day × 0.615 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/kWh = 273.7 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/day  ⇒  0.274 kg CO<sub>2e</sub>/m<sup>3</sup></span>.\n<em>(Hand calculation matches numerical solver outputs exactly within rounding error).</em></li>\n</ul>";
